# =============================================================================
# ik-llama-toolkit -- global defaults
# =============================================================================
# Values here apply to every model profile. A profile in config/models/*.env
# may override any of them, and an exported shell variable overrides both:
#
#   IK_CTX=131072 ./serve.sh          # one-off override
#
# Tuned for:
#   GPU  NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Workstation, 96 GiB, sm_120
#   CPU  Intel Core Ultra 7 270K Plus -- 8 P-cores (0-7) + 16 E-cores (8-23)
#   RAM  256 GB DDR5-6667 (4 x 64), dual channel, ~107 GB/s theoretical
#        244 GiB is what the OS sees; see RESULTS 26.3 for where the rest goes.
#        Earlier results were taken on other kits -- 224 GiB at 6267, and
#        2 x 48 at 7400 -- and say so where it matters (RESULTS 25, 26).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which profile config/models/<name>.env to load when none is given on the CLI.
#
# Since 2026-08-17 this is `gpu-experts` (RESULTS §21-§24): the experts that do
# not fit in VRAM are computed ON THE GPU rather than on the CPU, which is what
# happens as soon as -rtr is not passed. At 131072 context it measures
#
#   4k 1382 pp / 21.23 tg     16k 1910 / 21.07
#   32k 1846 / 20.22          128k 1362 / 18.00
#
# against 478 / 486 / 437 pp for the profile it replaces -- roughly 3x prefill,
# for 3-7 % less generation and no quality trade.
#
# PROVISIONAL. The NaN-logits abort that produced seven crashes over 2026-08-13/14
# is very likely an upstream f16 overflow in DSA attention, fixed in ff141691 and
# 7cd62a3e, which this build now has; 610k prefilled tokens of the shape that used
# to abort came back clean (§24). That is one synthetic run against seven aborts,
# so this default is on trial until a day of real traffic agrees with it.
#
# If it misbehaves, `deepseek-v4-flash-128k-kvram` is the previous default and
# `deepseek-v4-flash` is the --fit profile that simply comes up unattended. The
# original Step-3.7-Flash default is still there as `step-3.7-flash-q4`.
# Back to 128k on 2026-08-29 after measuring both: 262144 costs ~13 % of every
# turn (two more expert layers on the host plus a smaller -ub) to serve the 8 %
# of requests that run deep, and the deepest ever recorded here was 139 264.
# The 128k profile with --swa-compress gives that up for a 4 % penalty against
# the old -nkvo settings while being the fastest of all at depth. Hermes
# compacts above the limit. Full table in RESULTS 49.5.
#
# --- 2026-09-03: the default is now Qwen3.8-Flash-Next Q8_0 at 131072 --------
# Matt's call, so that Hermes serves from it. Measured in RESULTS 51:
#
#   2302.7 pp / 40.6 tg   at -ncmoe 17 -ub 2048, shallow
#
# Against the DeepSeek profile it replaces (1711.8 pp / 19.11 tg at ~6.4k) that
# is +35 % prefill and 2.1x generation, at a much larger quant.
#
# TWO THINGS TO KNOW BEFORE TRUSTING IT UNATTENDED:
#
#   1. Q8_0 leaves ~94 GiB of experts on the host, so it is the slowest of the
#      four Qwen profiles at generation. `qwen38-flash-next-q4km-128k` runs
#      3486 pp / 128.9 tg -- 3.2x the generation -- if quality allows it.
#   2. It has NOT been soaked. The DeepSeek default earned its place over weeks
#      of real traffic and a NaN-abort hunt (RESULTS 19-49); this one has a few
#      benchmark runs, validated to N_KV 75 776 of 131072. Treat the first days
#      of Hermes traffic as the soak.
#
# The previous default remains one flag away: `./serve.sh
# deepseek-v4-flash-gpu-experts-128k`, or set IK_PROFILE in the environment.
#
# --- 2026-09-04: back to DeepSeek, now with the mask-share patch -------------
# The build carries keep-mask-share.patch (RESULTS 49.10, TODO 17): CUDA0
# compute buffer 7040 -> 4992 MiB on this profile, +24 % prefill and +10 %
# generation at 122k; since the 04:35 build also keep-window-cpu.patch (49.11),
# 4992 -> 4744, throughput unchanged. The patches only run in build_deepseek4(),
# so the Qwen day never exercised them; this is their soak. Proof they are
# active is in the load log: `CUDA0 compute buffer size = 4744.03 MiB`
# (mask-share alone: 4991.80, old graph: 7040.03).
# --- 2026-09-05: back to Qwen3.8-Flash-Next Q8_0 at 131072 -------------------
# Matt's call again, so that Hermes serves from it. What changed since the
# 09-03 stint is upstream #2404 (RESULTS 52), in the build since fe215a8c: on
# qwen4exp, generation attends over the cells the sparse indexer selected
# instead of the whole cache, so it is nearly independent of depth. Measured on
# the Q4_K_M profile, 500 W: 92.1 -> 103.8 t/s at 32k, 62.2 -> 87.8 at 96k,
# 52.9 -> 81.3 at 129k, prefill unchanged, nothing below 6144 tokens where the
# gather does not engage. Measured on this Q8_0 profile the same evening
# (RESULTS 52.2, 600 W): 35.3 -> 37.1 t/s at 32k and 30.8 -> 34.3 at 76k -- a
# quarter of the Q4 gain, because with ~96 GiB of experts crossing PCIe per
# token this profile's decode was never bound by the KV cache. Shallow figures
# are unchanged, 2301 pp / 40.6 tg at -ncmoe 17 -ub 2048. That run is also the
# first to reach 129 024 of the 131 072 window with no error; 51's stopped at
# 75 776.
#
# The two caveats from 09-03 still stand: Q8_0 leaves ~94 GiB of experts on the
# host and is the slowest of the four Qwen profiles at generation
# (`qwen38-flash-next-q4km-128k` is 3.2x on generation if quality allows), and
# it still has no soak behind it -- treat Hermes traffic as the soak. The
# DeepSeek profile is one flag away: `./serve.sh
# deepseek-v4-flash-gpu-experts-128k`, and its patches (mask-share, window-cut)
# are dead code on this arch, so switching costs nothing but a reload.
: "${IK_PROFILE:=qwen38-flash-next-q8-128k}"

# --- server ------------------------------------------------------------------
: "${IK_HOST:=127.0.0.1}"
# 8090, not 8080: on this box the graffiti project's Caddy container maps
# 0.0.0.0:8080 -> 80 permanently, and LM Studio's API server also tends to take
# 8080 when running. A bare ./serve.sh on 8080 therefore dies with "couldn't
# bind to server socket". The DeepSeek wrappers already override to 8090; this
# makes the bare command work too.
: "${IK_PORT:=8090}"
: "${IK_ALIAS:=}"                 # model name reported by /v1/models; blank = file name
: "${IK_PARALLEL:=1}"             # concurrent slots; >1 splits the KV cache
: "${IK_API_KEY:=}"               # if set, clients must send this bearer token
: "${IK_JINJA:=1}"                # use the GGUF's embedded chat template
: "${IK_CONT_BATCHING:=1}"        # continuous batching (only matters if PARALLEL>1)

# --- context and KV cache ----------------------------------------------------
# Step-3.7-Flash trains to 262144. The KV cache is cheap here because only
# 1 layer in 4 uses full attention (the rest have a 512-token sliding window)
# and all layers use 8 KV heads, so raising this is inexpensive.
: "${IK_CTX:=65536}"

# Flash attention. Must be given as "on"/"off" in this build -- a bare -fa
# would swallow the next argument.
: "${IK_FA:=on}"

# q8_0 KV is visually lossless and halves cache size vs f16. Use f16 if you
# want to rule the cache out as a quality variable.
: "${IK_CTK:=q8_0}"
: "${IK_CTV:=q8_0}"

# --- where the models live -----------------------------------------------------
# IK_MODELS_ROOT is deliberately NOT set here. It is set in lib/common.sh before
# the profile is sourced, because the profile is sourced first and would not see
# a value declared in this file. Override it in the environment instead:
#   IK_MODELS_ROOT=/mnt/models ./serve.sh

# --- batching ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Larger micro-batches raise prompt-processing throughput at the cost of VRAM
# for compute buffers. 4096/1024 is a good hybrid CPU+GPU point; drop UBATCH
# to 512 if you hit an out-of-memory during warmup.
: "${IK_BATCH:=4096}"
: "${IK_UBATCH:=1024}"

# --- threads -----------------------------------------------------------------
# MEASURED (./bench.sh threads, re-swept 2026-08-10 on MXFP4 / ncmoe=16,
# measuring *both* metrics -- the 2026-07 sweep only measured tg, which is the
# metric threads do not affect, and stopped at 18 on the mistaken belief that
# this CPU has 18 cores rather than 24):
#
#   threads   12     16     18     20     22     24
#   pp512    268.6  316.7  333.4  350.8  343.0  354.6   <- +32% from 12 to 24
#   tg128    24.63  25.03  24.97  25.07  25.24  24.92   <- flat, bandwidth-bound
#
# Generation is pinned by DDR5 bandwidth, so cores past ~12 add nothing to it;
# prefill is matmul over the CPU-resident experts and keeps scaling. Both are
# set to all 24 cores because that is the prefill optimum and costs tg nothing.
# Pinning to the 8 P-cores measures *slower* -- the E-cores do real work at
# these splits. Re-run ./bench.sh threads if you change IK_CTX/IK_NCMOE a lot.
: "${IK_THREADS:=24}"
: "${IK_THREADS_BATCH:=24}"

# Optional hard pinning, e.g. IK_CPU_LIST=0-7 to force the P-cores (0-7; the
# E-cores are 8-23). Left empty: measured slower than letting Intel Thread
# Director place all 24 threads.
: "${IK_CPU_LIST:=}"

# --- expert placement (the single most important setting) --------------------
# The model is ~91% routed-expert weights, so "how much fits on the GPU" is
# decided almost entirely by how many layers of experts stay in system RAM.
#
# Three mutually exclusive modes, checked in this order:
#   IK_OT     explicit -ot regexes (full manual control)
#   IK_NCMOE  keep experts of the first N layers on the CPU
#   IK_FIT    let ik_llama measure free VRAM and decide (default)
#
# --fit accounts for weights, the per-layer KV cache (including the sliding
# window layers) and compute buffers, and re-decides on every launch, so it
# adapts if something else is using the GPU.
: "${IK_FIT:=1}"
: "${IK_FIT_MARGIN:=2048}"        # MiB of VRAM to leave free (desktop + headroom)
: "${IK_NCMOE:=}"                 # e.g. 17 -> experts of layers 0..16 on CPU
: "${IK_OT:=}"                    # e.g. 'blk\.(3|4|5)\.ffn_.*_exps\.weight=CPU'

# --- MoE / offload switches --------------------------------------------------
: "${IK_FMOE:=1}"                 # fused up+gate MoE op (on by default upstream)
: "${IK_OOAE:=1}"                 # transfer only activated experts to the GPU.
                                  # With 8 of 288 experts active this is a big
                                  # win at small batch sizes.
: "${IK_NGL:=99}"                 # offload all layers; expert tensors are then
                                  # pulled back to CPU by --fit/-ncmoe/-ot

# --- memory / loading --------------------------------------------------------
: "${IK_THP:=1}"                  # transparent huge pages for CPU-side tensors
: "${IK_RTR:=0}"                  # run-time repack CPU experts into the _R4/_R8
                                  # layout. That also picks the PROCESSOR: the
                                  # repacked types have no CUDA kernel, so the
                                  # CPU runs the expert GEMM instead of the GPU
                                  # streaming experts per micro-batch. Wins only
                                  # at small -ub: Step-3.7 +16% prefill at ub 512
                                  # (2026-07-25); DeepSeek at ub 4096 478 vs 1376
                                  # without it (RESULTS 21). Generation unchanged
                                  # either way. Forces --no-mmap (full re-read
                                  # per start). Never bake it into the file with
                                  # llama-quantize --repack: that pins the
                                  # experts to the CPU for good (TODO 8).
: "${IK_MLOCK:=0}"                # lock CPU tensors in RAM (needs ulimit -l)
: "${IK_NO_MMAP:=0}"
: "${IK_DEFER_EXPERTS:=0}"        # fault expert pages in lazily -> faster start,
                                  # slower first tokens
: "${IK_PREFETCH_EXPERTS:=0}"

# --- advanced / experimental -------------------------------------------------
: "${IK_AMB:=}"                   # cap K*Q scratch size in MiB, e.g. 512
: "${IK_SER:=}"                   # smart expert reduction "<Kmin>,<thresh>",
                                  # e.g. "6,1" forces 6 instead of 8 experts.
                                  # Faster, but changes model output -- opt in.
: "${IK_EXTRA_ARGS:=}"            # anything else, appended verbatim

# --- build -------------------------------------------------------------------
: "${IK_CUDA_ARCH:=120-real}"     # sm_120 = Blackwell RTX PRO 6000
: "${IK_BUILD_JOBS:=}"            # defaults to nproc
